WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.010
Governments and tech
giants are pouring money

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.360
into the wildest ideas

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.000 position:64%
for removing carbon dioxide from the air.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.670
Once you capture it, you
can bury it deep underground

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.280
or you can sink it into the sea.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.140
And some startups are even trying

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.650
to turn captured carbon
dioxide into products.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.690 position:59%
Jet fuel, laundry detergent with Tide,

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.703
and even sunglasses with Pangaia.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.310
I found that one of the biggest
carbon removal companies,

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.560
a Swiss startup called Climeworks,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.290
sells removed carbon to individuals

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.153
at more than $1,000 a ton.

00:00:37.845 --> 00:00:41.040
So that got me thinking, can
I buy some captured carbon

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.601
from any of these companies

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.098
and display it on my desk?

00:00:56.563 --> 00:00:58.020
All right, so, hi.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.240 position:61%
I understand you capture carbon dioxide

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.950
and store it somehow.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:02.820
Is that right?

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:03.653
That's exactly right.

00:01:03.653 --> 00:01:06.450
We capture CO2 from the
tailpipe of a semi-truck

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:07.860
with a retrofit device.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.370
So if I wanted to buy
some of that stored carbon

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.951
so that I can put it on my desk

00:01:12.951 --> 00:01:15.420
and show people that
it's a real technology,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.077
can I do that?

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:17.910
Unfortunately,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.640
we can't really share a
cylinder of liquid CO2.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.515
I'm not sending you a ton of CO2,

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:23.940
you realize?
No, I won't have space

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.390
for a ton of CO2, I'm afraid.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.010 position:58%
And do you have some of it right now?

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:29.880
I can go grab some.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.673
Should I go grab some?
That'd be great.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:33.660
Alrighty.
There you go.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.040
There you go.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.150
Show it to me a little bit.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.670
And so, like, what the
hell is that? Does it

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.890
I mean yes, it's black,
so maybe it's carbon,

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.300
but what is it?

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.213
This is bio-oil, and
it's very rich in carbon.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.680 position:59%
Can I get some of the captured carbon?

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.110
Unfortunately not, but for
the best reasons, right?

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.753 position:64%
It's captured as stone, it's mineralized.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:57.840
No.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.300
Okay, so you won't sell
me this captured carbon,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.690
but can I come and visit
you in Iceland and see it?

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.750
Absolutely, you're very
welcome to visit us anytime

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:07.950
and we will-
And touch it?

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.249
Can I touch it?

00:02:09.249 --> 00:02:10.224
Absolutely.

00:02:10.224 --> 00:02:11.057
Great.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.183
I expected to see a barren
landscape and some mountains,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.278
but seeing it from up
close is a thing of beauty.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.350
Under the Paris Agreement, all
the world's countries agreed

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.740
to keep global warming
below 2 degrees Celsius,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.880 position:66%
or if possible, below 1.5 degrees Celsius.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.710
Now, what does that translate to?

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:50.880
It means that the world needs

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.430 position:64%
to not just stop emitting carbon dioxide,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.690
which it is doing currently

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.990 position:58%
at nearly 40 billion tons every year,

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.360 position:61%
but also remove existing carbon dioxide

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.013
that's already in the atmosphere.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:07.200
An estimate suggests we will
need to capture and remove

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.530
2 billion to 10 billion
tons of carbon dioxide

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.390
from the air, every year,

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.026
for the rest of century,
starting from 2050,

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:19.823
somehow.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.720 position:63%
Iceland is the only country in the world

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.720 position:58%
where you can witness climate change,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.880 position:61%
the melting of the ice on the glaciers,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:33.753
firsthand,

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.423 position:61%
but you can also discover the solution.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.750
This is Olafur Ragnar Grímsson,

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.910
the former President of Iceland.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:48.090
The solution he's talking
about is one he facilitated

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.770
between two of the biggest players

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.671
in the carbon removal space:

00:03:51.671 --> 00:03:52.821
Climeworks and Carbfix.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.150
Oh, wow, this is stunning.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.020 position:59%
So we are surrounded by volcanic rocks

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.180
that have been solidified,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:11.010
and in the distance, we see
these big, huge towers of steam

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.410 position:56%
just going straight up into the air,

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:17.130
which I expect is the
geothermal power plant.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.110
This is energy that you can draw

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:22.560
from just beneath the
land here in Iceland,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.230 position:66%
not very deep, maybe a few hundred meters,

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:28.293
and you can convert it into
electricity or just heat.

00:04:31.448 --> 00:04:36.180
We are headed to go and see the
biggest startup in the world

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.070 position:63%
that removes carbon dioxide from the air

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.720
and then turns it into stone

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.460
here in Iceland.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.033
The reason these startups
are here is because of this,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.790 position:56%
geothermal power that is carbon-free

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.650
so that they can do the work

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:55.087 position:59%
to remove carbon dioxide from the air.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.760
So what are we looking at?

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.110
So this is the world's first

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.030
direct air capture and storage unit

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.600 position:56%
called Orca, operated by Climeworks.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.740
It is the first of its kind,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.320
and it's also the largest
direct air capture unit

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.153
in the globe.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.830
So the whole process works

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:18.540
that you have six chambers
on each container-sized unit.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.390 position:63%
So each chamber has a specialized filter

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:24.060
and vents that pull in
the air from the outside.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.793
So you have the ambient
air flowing through there,

00:05:27.657 --> 00:05:29.850
the CO2 is captured in the filter,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.540
and the cleaner air
leaves directly, right?

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.930
But what do you do when
the filter is full?

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.179
Well, then we have to
close the compartment and-

00:05:39.179 --> 00:05:40.137
And then what's happening

00:05:40.137 --> 00:05:40.988
behind the closed compartment?

00:05:40.988 --> 00:05:41.821
When it's closed,

00:05:41.821 --> 00:05:43.140
behind the closed doors,

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.040
we apply heat, so around
100 degrees Celsius, so-

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.540
And that releases the CO2?

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:51.000
This releases the CO2,
which is then captured

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.940
and vacuum sucked back
into the processing hole

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.170
where it's further processed,

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.660
so it's 100% CO2,

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.480
and then we send it through
an underground pipeline

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.707
over to Carbfix

00:06:01.707 --> 00:06:02.886
for the mineralization process.

00:06:02.886 --> 00:06:04.640
Carbfix is-

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.683
Is further down that way.
Okay.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.060
The surprising thing, which
I kind of knew going in,

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.340
but when you see it,
it really strikes you,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.663
is that the world's largest
carbon removal plant is tiny.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.130
It's tiny in the sense of the
grand scale of things, right?

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.310
Like, this plant is doing
something that's science fiction,

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.530
and it's doing it in a place where

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.410
you get to see a large
geothermal power plant

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.900 position:56%
that's pumping out big steam plumes.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.270
There's like real activity
happening elsewhere.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.720
If you look at the actual
facility capturing carbon,

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:43.770
it's a small, tiny thing
sitting in the corner,

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.173 position:66%
not doing anything as far as you can tell.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.470
The plant captures 4,000 tons
of carbon dioxide a year,

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.420
but that only makes up
the annual emissions

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:57.693
of about 250 US residents.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.140
The important bit is
that it's the first step

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.180
in a scale-up process.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.740
So what's next?

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.930
Well, you can actually see
it here, right next door.

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.900 position:56%
This is the construction of Mammoth,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.400
which is the next Orca-type
plant in the scale-up process,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.000
and this will capture
36,000 tons of CO2 per year.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.790
Almost nine times as much.
Yes.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:24.840
But right now, the problem
is 40 billion tons of CO2

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.130
being pumped into the air.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:29.760
Yeah, we need to reduce
emissions first and foremost.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.630
This is not a magic solution
that will remove that problem.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:34.740
So what we need to do

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:38.460
is, like, 90% reduce
emissions drastically,

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:39.840
and then this is 10%.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.820
This is for the historic emissions.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:44.730
This is for the unavoidable
emissions in the future.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.350
So not a magic bullet,

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.900 position:59%
but a part of the arsenal that we have

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.100
to fight climate change.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:01.563
We are inside a lava tunnel.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.650
About 5,000 years ago,

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.250
there was a lava
explosion here in Iceland.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:12.660 position:61%
And when volcanoes erupt and cool down,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.720 position:66%
they leave behind lots and lots of basalt.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.140 position:64%
So all this rock is predominantly basalt,

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.920
that's pretty much what
all of Iceland is made of,

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:27.063
and basalt rock is really good
at capturing carbon dioxide.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:34.530
Basalt is made of lots of
salts from different metals,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.720
iron, magnesium, calcium,

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.480
and when carbon dioxide
reacts with calcium,

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.400
it forms calcium carbonate

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:44.316
and it traps the gas as a rock.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.440
Carbfix imitates and accelerates
these natural processes

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.713
to permanently store carbon
dioxide in basaltic rock.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.793
These futuristic pods
is where it all happens.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.690 position:61%
So here we are actually doing something

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.820
for the first time in the world

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:27.870
in collaboration with our
friends at Climeworks.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.230
They are capturing CO2
directly from the atmosphere,

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:34.200
which we then take and
inject into the ground

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.150
and turn it into stone.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:40.053
And how do they move
their CO2 to your system?

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:46.230 position:56%
Once they have collected enough CO2,

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.960 position:59%
it's piped towards our injection well,

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:49.983
just as this one.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.850 position:66%
And there, we co-inject the CO2 with water

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.390
to ensure that the CO2 gets
dissolved in the water.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:00.510 position:66%
And once the CO2 is dissolved in the fluid

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.700
and we have the sparkling water,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.803
we inject it into the bedrock.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.670
There, the sparkling water
reacts with the bedrock

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.310 position:56%
and dissolves metals out of the rock

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.490
that combine with the CO2
and turn it into stone.

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.410
So when you inject CO2 underground,

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:20.117
you can actually now show
us what exactly happens.

00:10:20.117 --> 00:10:24.810 position:58%
Yeah, this is actually a nice example

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.090
of kind of before and after.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:28.290
Yeah, can I touch it?

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.050
Of course.
Ooh, it is rocklike,

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.150 position:59%
but it also feels a little soft to me,

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.540
like it feels like I'm
gonna be able to crumble it.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.450
You won't .
Ah, no, I won't, okay.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:41.520
Now, this is basalt, right?

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.830 position:56%
Yes, it kind of looks like a sponge,

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:47.850
and these pores, and
fractures, and vesicles,

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.550
they can actually host the CO2

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:52.230
once you've turned it into stone.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:54.720
And that's what you see here.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.040
In this rock, we've
already stored some CO2.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.250
You can see all these white spots.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.890 position:59%
So here, the pores are actually filled

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.420
with these white minerals,

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:09.303
and this is just simply
CO2 turned to stone.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:15.903
How long does that stay
in the form of stone?

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:18.360
Forever.
Oh, really?

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:19.830
Yeah, we are talking

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:21.980
about geological timescales there.

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:27.090
Okay, so you're gonna-

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:29.850
the bottle you'll send me,
it has to be tightly sealed.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:31.020
Yeah.
Okay.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:34.560
But can you show me the process
of how it actually works?

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:35.869
Yeah, let's walk out back.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:46.350 position:63%
Alrighty, well, this is the corn stover,

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:49.980 position:66%
the agricultural residue of choice for us.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:53.730
And it's a mixture of
things like corn cobs

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:58.730
as well as the stalks and
leaves of corn plants.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:02.460 position:58%
Capturing carbon dioxide from the air

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.620
isn't the only way to do it.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:07.680
A lot of carbon dioxide is
naturally captured by plants.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.680
The problem is the plants
release it back into the air

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:11.913
when they die and rot.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:15.450
A 100 million acres of
corn grown every year

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:16.680
all over the US

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.133
that's capturing something
like 600 million tons of CO2.

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.920
About 95% of it rots on
the field every year,

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:24.840
and it's a shame, we just
let it rot on the field

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:25.673
and all that CO2

00:12:25.673 --> 00:12:27.120
that we've already done
the hard work of capturing

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:28.590
by doing all this plant growing

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:30.653
just goes straight back
into the atmosphere.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.850
Charm Industrial says

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:34.740
why not take these dead plants

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:36.950
and bury their captured
carbon in the ground

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:38.223
so it can't escape?

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:44.640
So today, we have farmers
take the cornstalks

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:45.720
up off their field,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.660
rake them into windrows, bale them,

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.460
transport the bales to
their edge of the field,

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:51.930
then load the bales onto a truck

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.770
and drive those bales to
one of our test sites.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:59.670
So once the biomass arrives
at one of our pyrolyzers,

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:01.920 position:63%
it goes through first a step of grinding

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:05.700
to less than half an inch in size.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:08.640
And then those very
small pieces of biomass

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:12.120
get very rapidly heated
to about 500 degrees C,

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:15.540
and that effectively
vaporizes the biomass.

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.010
And so a really important
thing to understand

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:20.850
is that the pyrolyzer's
energy to drive that heat

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.470
is coming from the biomass itself,

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:26.040
it's not consuming power off
grid or something like that.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.800
Aside from a little bit
of propane at startup,

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.060
it has then become self-sustaining

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:31.833
on the energy in the biomass.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:33.893
And from there then,

00:13:33.893 --> 00:13:37.020
we separate out, first,
the solid char particles.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.340
This what goes back into the field,

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:40.110
improves the soil health.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.090
Then that vapor stream
continues to the next step

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:45.273
where we condense the bio-oil out.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:51.960
This is the bio-oil.

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:53.940
So this is the barbecue sauce,

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:55.710
and that barbecue sauce,

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:58.140
it literally is the natural
smoke flavor ingredient

00:13:58.140 --> 00:13:59.223
in barbecue sauce,

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:03.030
is very rich in carbon,
and it's a dense fluid,

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:08.030
and we can inject that into
old oil and gas reserves.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.740 position:63%
In the US today, there's about 2 million

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:14.010
end-of-life oil and gas wells,

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:15.630 position:64%
places where we extracted oil previously,

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:17.100
so in many ways, we're
sort of putting oil back

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:18.050
where it came from.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:23.490
Late last year, Charm
announced the removal

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:28.260
of about 5,500 tons of
carbon dioxide so far.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:30.600
None of these companies are
capturing carbon dioxide

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:32.880 position:56%
on a scale to make much of an impact

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:34.863
on addressing climate change today.

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:40.650
The industry must remove
tens of billions of tons

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:42.810
from the atmosphere this century,

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:43.890
which will require growth

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:45.320
that eclipses even that

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:47.400 position:61%
of the computer and software industries

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:48.693
in recent decades.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:53.520
So carbon removal technology,
it's not that difficult.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:55.950 position:56%
The ideas are quite basic chemistry,

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:58.140
quite basic chemical engineering,

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.353 position:59%
and all they really need now is money.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.140
So far, most of the money has gone

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.660 position:59%
towards electricity and for transport.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.810
Very little money has gone
towards carbon capture.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:12.720
So currently, some hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:15.300
are being spent on these
startups, like Climeworks,

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:16.410
every year.

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:17.670
That number will have to go

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:20.910
into the billions of dollars
or tens of billions of dollars

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:22.440
to be able to make an impact

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:25.320
on actually removing lots
and lots of carbon dioxide

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:26.280
from the air,

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:29.403
and the more you do it,
the cheaper it becomes.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:32.880
Some governments, including the US,

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.470
are finally starting to step up

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.990 position:58%
and put money towards carbon capture,

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:39.510
but most of that money,
currently, is coming

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:41.070
from large corporations

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:43.353 position:59%
wanting to reach those net zero goals.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:55.260 position:56%
There is proof right here on my desk

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:57.390
that this technology can work.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:59.613 position:59%
We just need to make it work at scale.

